# Do not delete this file, and do not delete this directory.
#
# It is not a placeholder for keys: the JWT signing keys live in `docker/keys/`
# and are mounted here. `docker-compose.{dev,staging}.yml` bind-mounts
# `../backend:/app` AND, nested inside it, `./keys:/app/keys` — so THIS
# directory is the host-side mountpoint of that second mount.
#
# Docker creates it at container-create time, empty and untracked, which made it
# invisible to `git status` (git does not track empty directories) and fair game
# for anything that cleans untracked paths: `git clean -fd`, `git stash -u`, an
# editor's "clean workspace". Remove it while the stack is up and the nested
# mount is torn down inside the running container's namespace — `docker inspect`
# still lists the mount, because it reads the config, but `/app/keys` is gone.
#
# The backend then keeps serving until its next restart (AIR rebuilds within
# minutes) and comes back with `JWT KEYS NOT LOADED - AUTHENTICATION WILL NOT
# WORK!`: `/health` and `/v1/auth/operator/{policy,providers}` still answer 200
# while `GET /v1/auth/session` answers 503, because only the session path needs
# to sign. A `docker restart` does not fix it — the mount is re-applied only
# when the container is recreated (`up -d --force-recreate backend`).
#
# Tracking this file pins the mountpoint so no git operation can take it away.
